<!--
beet's own entry: the dev commands, wired as routes over the capabilities linked
into the `beet` binary.

The wasm test runner depends on it: `.cargo/config.toml` sets
`runner = 'beet run-wasm'`, so `cargo test --target wasm32-...` invokes
`beet run-wasm <module>`, which discovers this entry and dispatches the
`<RunWasm/>` route. The rest are convenience: serve, build, check, static export,
pdf, s3 sync.

It is a one-shot `<CliServer always=true {CallOnLoad}>` root with the `Router` as its
child: the binary supplies only the load context (the process request), the root's
`CallOnLoad` calls with it on load, it hops down to the router, the positional route
arg selects the command, and the command resolves and the process exits.
`always=true` makes this dispatcher run on every boot regardless of `--server`, since
`--server` selects the *served site's* servers, not this entry's. The one exception
to exiting is `<Serve/>`, which loads a site and boots *its* servers, parking so the
process serves.

    cargo install --path crates/beet-cli --all-features   # then, from anywhere in the repo:
    beet run-wasm <module.wasm>            # run a wasm test module
    beet check examples/bsx_site           # lint a no-code site
    beet export-static examples/bsx_site   # write its dist/
    just build-wasm-min                    # the smallest browser runtime (assets/wasm/beet-min.wasm)
    just build-wasm-full                   # ..and the kitchen sink (assets/wasm/beet-full.wasm)

The two sites in this repo both serve through `<Serve/>` (`<site>` is a directory
holding an entry document, or the entry file itself):

    beet serve site                        # the beet website (site/main.bsx), http
    beet serve examples/bsx_site           # a zero-code BSX site, http
    beet serve site/main.bsx               # ..the entry file resolves the same site
    beet serve site --server=tui           # ..as a live terminal (like `beet present`)
    beet serve site --server=http,ssh      # ..http plus a multi-tenant ssh terminal

The beet website's own deploy does NOT live here: `site/main.bsx` declares the
site's resources and its deploy verbs, so the application that runs on them is
the thing that declares them (`just beet-deploy` runs `beet --main=site deploy`).
What lives here is this WORKSPACE's own shared asset bucket
(`beet--shared--assets`), the source of record for the files too large for git
that the examples, tests and wasm builds read out of `./assets`. It is
public-read so a fresh checkout hydrates without credentials, and it is declared
under a `shared`-stage `<DeployHost>` because a source shared by developers is
not owned by any deploy stage.

    just beet-shared apply                 # provision the workspace assets bucket
    just beet-shared pull                  # hydrate ./assets from it (no credentials)
    just beet-shared push                  # publish ./assets to it

`bx:features="infra,extra"` gates that subtree on the compiled feature set, so a
lean `beet` (the default build, `beet run-wasm`, `beet serve`) skips it before
its tags resolve rather than failing on an unregistered tag.

The shared verbs need AWS credentials in `.env`, plus `tofu`.
-->
<CliServer always=true {CallOnLoad}>
<Router {HelpHandler}>
	<PackageConfig title="Beet" app_name="beet" description="A creative tool engine built on bevy" version="0.0.9-dev.16"/>
	<Serve/>
	<RunWasm/>
	<BuildWasm/>
	<Check/>
	<AnalyticsReport/>
	<ExportStatic/>
	<ExportPdf/>
	<CaptureScreenshot/>
	<SyncS3/>
	<Fragment bx:features="infra,extra">
		<Route path="shared">
			<DeployHost stage="shared">
				<S3BucketBlock label="assets" deploy_versioned=false public_read=true force_destroy=false/>
				<Route path="push" {ExchangeSequence}>
					<DirSync bucket="assets" local_dir="assets"/>
				</Route>
				<Route path="pull" {ExchangeSequence}>
					<DirSync bucket="assets" local_dir="assets" {SyncS3Bucket{direction:Pull, no_sign_request:true}}/>
				</Route>
			</DeployHost>
		</Route>
	</Fragment>
</Router>
</CliServer>
